sing moves:  \n- **Pair ♠10 ♥10**: Middle pair, safe, keeps high cards.  \n- **Pair ♥K ♣K**: High pair, could pressure landlord if they don’t have a pair of 2s or higher.  \n- **Straight (♥10 ♥J ♣Q ♣K ♠A)**: Strong sequence that landlord can only beat with a bomb or higher straight (unlikely given remaining cards). Clears five cards from my hand.  \n- **Single ♠2**: Highest single, but wastes my best single early, and landlord might pass to see more of my hand.  \n\nThe **straight** is appealing because:  \n- It’s a strong multi-card combination that reduces my hand size significantly (from 12 to 7 cards).  \n- Given remaining unknown cards, a straight J-A is very hard to beat unless landlord has a bomb—but if they do, they’d need to use it now, revealing power early.  \n- Even if they bomb it, I still have a pair of 4s and ♠2 as potential finishers later.  \n- If they pass, I gain initiative and can continue with smaller pairs/singles, putting pressure.  \n\n**Reason for choice:**  \nThe straight is likely unbeatable by single/pair/triplet plays, forcing landlord to either pass or use a bomb. If they bomb, that uses 4 of their last 4 cards—leaving them empty if they had only bomb, or if not, they’ll have very few cards left and I can still coordinate with teammate. If they pass, I improve my position greatly. Risk is if they have a higher straight (needs 10–A with all same suits/ranks available—unlikely given known cards). Best aggressive reduction move.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"cards": ["♥10", "♥J", "♣Q", "♣K", "♠A"]}'}
